<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Pontianak Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1771 CE – 1950 CE · capital Pontianak</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Arab-founded Malay sultanate at the mouth of the Kapuas river in West Borneo, origin of the city of Pontianak.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syarif-abdurrahman-alkadrie">Syarif Abdurrahman Alkadrie</a>, son of a Hadhrami Arab cleric who had settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mempawah">Mempawah</a>, built a trading settlement at the junction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapuas-river">Kapuas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landak-river">Landak</a> rivers in 1771. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> support he made it a thriving port and was proclaimed sultan in 1778, founding a dynasty of Arab descent.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Pontianak controlled the trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapuas-river">Kapuas</a> basin, exporting gold, diamonds, rattan and forest products. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a> recognised the sultan in 1779 in return for a share of trade. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kadriah-palace">Kadriah</a> palace and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jami">Jami</a> mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syarif-abdurrahman-alkadrie">Syarif Abdurrahman</a> survive in the city.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>During the Japanese occupation <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syarif-muhammad-alkadrie">Syarif Muhammad Alkadrie</a> and much of the West Bornean elite were executed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandor-affair">Mandor killings</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamid-ii-of-pontianak">Sultan Hamid II</a>, who designed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garuda-pancasila">Garuda Pancasila</a> emblem, led West Kalimantan within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/united-states-of-indonesia">United States of Indonesia</a>, but was arrested in 1950 and the sultanate was absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/pontianak-sultanate.jpg" alt="Pontianak Sultanate" width="320" height="256"><figcaption>Image: Dre.comandante · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Pontianak_Sultanate.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1771 CE – 1950 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pontianak</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Syarif Abdurrahman Alkadrie, Hamid II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Pontianak, Kadriah Sultanate, Pontianak</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1860&amp;focus=pontianak-sultanate" aria-label="Pontianak Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7717 2178 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Pontianak Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8037 2434L7959 2663M8037 2434L7959 2663M8037 2434L7959 2664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7959 2663h0M7959 2663h0M7959 2664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8037 2434h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8037 2434h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Pontianak. <a href="/atlas?year=1860&amp;focus=pontianak-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
